{"type": "thinking", "text": "Confirmed quantity 3 shows +18 XP; exact 18.6. Element 22 current, quote \"+18 XP total\".\n\nNow file a report focused on fractional truncation. Expected should be \"+18.6 XP total\" or \"+19 XP total\". Which to choose? The app's XP are integers, but the underlying recipe is 6.2 XP/bar (evidence: 25 bars shows 155 = 25×6.2). The correct total is 18.6; a formatter that rounds would show \"+19\". The simplified formatter truncates to \"+18\". I'll say expected \"+18.6 XP total\" (or at least +19 rounded), observed \"+18\". This is defensible.\n\nBut be careful: the developer may again reject. Let's frame it as \"XP total is truncated, not rounded: 3 Bronze Bars = 18.6 XP but readout shows 18.\" Maybe they'll confirm. If not, no harm.\n\nLet's file. element_ref 22 (current observation), observed_quote \"+18 XP total\". screen \"Smithing > Bronze Bar > crafting batch dialog\". field \"XP total readout\". symptom wrong_value."}
{"type": "text", "text": "I've reproduced a second aspect of the readout formatter: fractional XP totals are truncated. Filing this separately."}
